tings, or fundraising figures exist yet", "The competitive Senate race — markets favor the Democrat near 61% while one poll showed the Republican back in front — signals a contestable Maine electorate", "Open-seat race with a term-limited incumbent removes any incumbency advantage for either side"], "rationale": "With no polls, ratings, or market pricing for the presidential race itself, the forecast rests on Maine's roughly four-point Democratic lean. The Senate contest shows Republicans can compete statewide, but presidential voting in Maine has run more Democratic than that, so the state's fundamental edge holds.", "watch": "Early 2028 state-level presidential polling, or a decisive shift in the Senate race's trajectory, could quickly move this open-seat forecast.", "confidence": "low", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "Maine's 2028 presidential contest is an open race with no incumbent on the ballot, and no public general-election polls, expert ratings, or candidate fundraising are yet available. The state's recent partisan lean of roughly four points Democratic stands as the main quantitative signal, supporting a modest Democratic edge with unusually wide uncertainty.\n\nFederal results in Maine remain genuinely contestable: prediction markets put the Democratic Senate nominee near 61 percent even as one recent poll showed the Republican senator back in front, a reminder that statewide margins can compress. With no race-specific evidence to justify a move, the forecast holds near the state's fundamental lean and treats the outcome as competitive but tilted Democratic.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.679, "margin": 4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.642, "margin": 4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.6494, "margin": 4.0}